{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-ba5ac8b7-41b6-40c4-bb5a-ff784fc7a7d0","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-98c069dfdf8ce6cc","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-vwzxu9gt/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8009 — Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n\nTriage (Step 0): not below-threshold (M-L effort, 4-7 sprints; three affected components;\nmultiple architecturally distinct work streams: MLflow server, MLflow Operator split-server\ntopology, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins extension, plus cross-component integration validation)\nand not documentation-only (all components require code changes). Full decomposition follows.\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Overrides (Step 1.5)\n\nThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. Three overrides\nwere applied and are traceable in the epic scope below:\n\n1. **No new flag — combine existing flags.** The AI-generated proposal for a new\n   `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag and `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field\n   is superseded: the decomposition implements composability of the existing\n   `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` flags. No epic introduces a new server flag\n   or a workspace-aware CR field.\n2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle.** The operator epics deploy a\n   dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and\n   HTTPRoute and configure artifact traffic routing — a split-server topology, not a single\n   Helm values propagation.\n3. **SSAR detail is over-specified.** No epic prescribes SelfSubjectAccessReview or other\n   Kubernetes API mechanics for workspace authorization; the plugin epic reuses the existing\n   auth plugin infrastructure, and the kube-rbac-proxy / `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup\n   is explicitly unchanged.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Component | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|-----------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Validate plugin workspace-resolution independence from tracking store initialization | Investigation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Validate artifact-path-to-workspace resolvability in the artifacts-only server | Investigation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Extend mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution for the artifacts-only context | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Determine split-server artifact traffic routing design | Investigation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | Operator deployment of the dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E007 | Operator configuration of artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E008 | Determine the audit logging channel for workspace access denials | Investigation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P2 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E009 | Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P2 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E010 | End-to-end integration validation of workspace-aware split-server artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E011 | Tracking-server artifact delegation configuration (conditional, branch B) | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P1 |\n\n10 main-plan epics plus 1 conditional branch epic. Branch epics are excluded from the\nmain-plan critical path and are not standalone Jira issues; BRANCH-B-E011 is attached to\nthe gating investigation (E005) as a plan document.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 INV: plugin init independence\"]\n    E002[\"E002 INV: path resolvability\"]\n    E003[\"E003 IMPL: plugin extension\"]\n    E004[\"E004 IMPL: server workspace-aware artifacts-only\"]\n    E005[\"E005 INV: routing design\"]\n    E006[\"E006 IMPL: dedicated instance deployment\"]\n    E007[\"E007 IMPL: artifact traffic routing\"]\n    E008[\"E008 INV: audit channel\"]\n    E009[\"E009 IMPL: audit logging\"]\n    E010[\"E010 IMPL: E2E integration validation\"]\n    E011B[\"BRANCH-B-E011 IMPL: server delegation config (conditional)\"]\n\n    E001 --> E003\n    E002 --> E004\n    E003 --> E004\n    E005 --> E006\n    E005 --> E007\n    E005 --> E011B\n    E004 --> E009\n    E008 --> E009\n    E004 --> E010\n    E006 --> E010\n    E007 --> E010\n```\n\nCritical path (main plan): E001 → E003 → E004 → E010 (4 epics).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The plugin-coupling finding rewrites the plugin epic's scope (verify-only vs. refactor of the initialization path). True gate: `gated_by=E001`, `gate_failure_impact=rewrite`. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 3 | The path-resolvability finding determines the server's workspace-resolution approach; if paths do not carry resolvable experiment/run identity, the approach is redesigned. True gate: `gated_by=E002`, `gate_failure_impact=rewrite`. |\n| E003 → E004 | Rule 12/13 (framework/library → consumer) | The server's workspace validation builds against the plugin's workspace resolution (loaded via the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point); the consumer serializes after the library it calls. |\n| E005 → E006 | Rule 3 | The routing-design outcome determines the HTTPRoute design for the dedicated instance. True gate: `gated_by=E005`, `gate_failure_impact=rewrite`. |\n| E005 → E007 | Rule 3 | The routing mechanism (gateway-level vs. server-side delegation) determines what the routing epic implements. True gate: `gated_by=E005`, `gate_failure_impact=rewrite`. |\n| E005 → BRANCH-B-E011 | Rule 3 (conditional branch) | The branch epic exists only in outcome B (tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations). `branch=B`, `gated_by=E005`, `gate_failure_impact.action=remove`. |\n| E004 → E009 | Rule 13 | Audit logging extends the workspace-validation denial points the server work creates (same code surface, built after it exists). |\n| E008 → E009 | Rule 3 | The channel decision (server log vs. OTLP vs. Kubernetes audit log) determines the audit epic's scope. True gate: `gated_by=E008`, `gate_failure_impact=rewrite`. |\n| E004 → E010 | Verification ordering | End-to-end validation exercises the workspace-aware server capability; it cannot run before the capability exists. |\n| E006 → E010, E007 → E010 | Verification ordering | End-to-end validation exercises the operator-deployed split-server topology (dedicated instance + routing). |\n| No edge: E004 ∥ E006/E007 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared artifact) | The operator renders flag combinations into a Deployment spec — a configuration reference to the server's capability, not a build-against artifact. The coupling (\"dedicated instance runs the workspace-aware flag combination\") is enforced by the operator epic's own acceptance criterion on the rendered container command, never by a DAG edge. |\n| No edge: E006 ∥ E007 | Rule 15 (same repo, different areas) | New artifacts-only chart templates vs. modification of the main tracking deployment's routing configuration; merge conflicts are coordination risk, not dependency. |\n| No edge: E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E005 ∥ E008 | Independent investigations | Four distinct unknowns resolved by different experiments (plugin source analysis, server artifact-path analysis, routing design analysis, PM/Security channel decision); each produces a different deliverable, so they are not combined. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1: `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | |\n| HLR-2: artifact requests validated against workspace membership; 403 on non-membership | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | |\n| HLR-3: fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | |\n| HLR-4: single-tenant / no-workspace deployments retain current behavior | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Same priority as HLR-1..3; same flag-gating and initialization code path — one epic, no split needed. |\n| HLR-5: operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only instance (Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute) | P1 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | |\n| HLR-6: main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | P1 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E007 (+ RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E011 in outcome B) | |\n| HLR-7: auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context without tracking store init | P1 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | E003 is P0 by priority inheritance: it transitively enables the P0 server HLRs 1-3. |\n| HLR-8: audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs | P2 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E009 | Split from the P0 server work rather than bundled: it is a deferrable feature with independent value (audit trail), and its scope is undetermined pending the channel decision — bundling would couple P0 delivery to a PM/Security decision. The split epic extends the denial path after it exists. |\n\nFull coverage: every HLR (P0/P1/P2) maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Open Question Disposition\n\n| Strategy unknown | Disposition |\n|------------------|-------------|\n| OQ: plugin workspace-store initialization vs. tracking store availability (MLflow Team) | Investigation E001 — answer changes plugin epic scope (verify vs. refactor). |\n| OQ: operator artifact traffic routing design; server-side change needed? (MLflow Team) | Investigation E005 — answer changes HTTPRoute design and conditionally adds the server-side delegation epic (branch B). Bounded outcomes: (A) gateway-level routing only, (B) server delegation change needed. |\n| OQ: audit channel for workspace access denials (PM/Security) | Investigation E008 — answer changes audit epic scope. |\n| OQ: acceptable latency overhead per artifact request (PM/Engineering) | Acceptance criterion on E004 (measure and document per-request overhead), not an investigation: performance optimization of artifact serving is explicitly out of scope (separate RFE), so the answer changes no epic's existence or approach here. See Health Warnings. |\n| Assumption: flags composable without breaking individual-flag initialization | Folded into E004 scope/ACs (implementation detail the team resolves doing the work) with E002 checking the interaction constraints. |\n| Assumption: workspace store provides experiment-to-workspace mapping without tracking stores | Investigation E001 (same experiment as the plugin-coupling question). |\n| Assumption: single-tenant deployments don't set `--enable-workspaces` | Regression scenario in E004 ACs and E010. |\n| Assumption: operator Helm chart supports a second Deployment without structural changes | Folded into E006 scope (chart architecture already renders multiple resource kinds; the epic verifies the second Deployment renders). |\n| Assumption: artifact request paths carry experiment/run identity | Investigation E002 — if false, the server's resolution approach is redesigned. |\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Epic size (E004)**: the strategy estimates the server-side flag composition and workspace\n  validation at 1-2 sprints; at the top of that range the epic runs above the ~2-week\n  guideline. It is kept as one epic because flag composition, workspace-provider\n  initialization, per-request validation, and fail-closed handling all edit the same server\n  initialization sequence and artifact request path — splitting would double-touch the same\n  call sites. **Re-split trigger**: re-split along initialization-composition vs.\n  request-validation lines if implementation exceeds the 2-sprint estimate or the validation\n  logic grows materially beyond the S3-scoped `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` path\n  resolution.\n- **E010 priority**: the end-to-end validation epic is P1, not P0. Its gating deliverable is\n  verification of the P1 operator topology (HLR-5/6); the P0 server behaviors carry their own\n  acceptance scenarios (strategy ACs 1-4) inside E004. Labeling E010 P0 would force the P1\n  operator epics to P0 through priority inheritance, misrepresenting the strategy's priority\n  labels.\n- **Component vocabulary mapping**: `.context/rhai-components.txt` has no entries for\n  \"MLflow Operator\" or \"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\"; every epic uses the canonical name\n  **MLflow** (the closest canonical name covering this component family, owned by the same\n  MLflow Team). `submit.py` would silently strip non-canonical names. Non-obvious mappings:\n  MLflow Operator → MLflow; mlflow-kubernetes-plugins → MLflow.\n- **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins has no architecture-context file** (`.context/architecture-context/`\n  contains mlflow.md, mlflow-operator.md, kube-rbac-proxy.md, PLATFORM.md only). The strategy's\n  dependency table marks it internal and existing, and mlflow.md documents the\n  `kubernetes-auth` plugin (v1.5.0), the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point, and the\n  workspace store — so no onboarding/provisioning chain was generated — but the plugin's\n  current initialization path is unverified by architecture context. E001 exists precisely to\n  validate it; treat plugin-side claims as strategy-asserted until E001 resolves.\n- **Latency/caching tension**: the risk table proposes short-TTL caching of workspace\n  membership decisions, while Out-of-Scope excludes performance optimization of artifact\n  serving (separate RFE). The decomposition follows the out-of-scope boundary: no caching\n  epic; E004 measures and documents per-request validation overhead so the follow-up RFE (or\n  a launch-blocking decision from the open latency question) has data.\n- **Strategy-level prerequisites pending human review** (Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build\n  Onboarding, Licence Validation, Accelerator/Package Support, Documentation Support, UXD\n  Support, Performance Team Support). No onboarding epics were generated: mlflow and\n  mlflow-operator are in the platform with existing Konflux pipelines per architecture\n  context; no new repos or images are introduced. The pending Architecture Review is\n  reflected in E004's human-process-gate signal.\n- **Design doc / ADR \"to be created\"** per the strategy's Supporting Documentation; the\n  routing-design investigation (E005) is the natural input to that design doc.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNo external dependency has uncertain timing — the strategy's dependency table lists all four\ndependencies as internal and existing (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API,\nkube-rbac-proxy). No Tier 1/Tier 2 split is required. Note, however, a natural phased\ndelivery emerges from the DAG: the server-side capability (E001-E004) delivers independent\npartial value for manually configured deployments, while the operator topology (E005-E007,\nE010) lands afterwards — this is verification/feature phasing, not external-dependency\ngating.\n\n## Conditional Branches\n\nThe routing-design investigation (E005) has two bounded outcomes:\n\n- **Branch A — gateway-level routing suffices**: the main tracking deployment routes\n  artifact traffic entirely through HTTPRoute/gateway configuration; no tracking-server\n  change is needed. No additional epic; the main-plan routing epic (E007) implements it.\n- **Branch B — tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact\n  operations**: in addition to E007, `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E011` (tracking-server\n  artifact delegation configuration) is planned. The branch file sets `branch=B`,\n  `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8009-E005` (also in its `dependencies`), and\n  `gate_failure_impact.action=remove` — if outcome A holds, this work is not needed.\n\nBranch epics are plan documents attached to the gating investigation, not standalone Jira\nissues; no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its dependencies.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":76067,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bc9990585c0fc227","agent_id":"a9ee74de494750547","session_id":"40ac0a95-f7b4-4f3c-bf55-b2c4e97b3f62","uuid":"83a386a9-671e-49f6-bdfa-ae5a1a689d0f","timestamp":"2026-10-25T18:21:35.366Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
