{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-7b50b1f9-cc8a-456b-b63a-f1289833b4fd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-93706128e0bbd8ba","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-vwzxu9gt/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E004.md","content":"# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the core server-side capability: `--artifacts-only` becomes composable\nwith the existing `--enable-workspaces` flag (no new server flag is introduced — Staff\nEngineer input). When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit\n`--workspace-store-uri` and initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate\nartifact requests; tracking stores and model registry stores stay uninitialized and\nnon-artifact API endpoints remain disabled. For each artifact request, the server resolves\nthe artifact path to its parent experiment, looks up the experiment's workspace assignment\nvia the workspace store, and delegates authorization to the existing auth plugin\ninfrastructure. The design is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable, artifact\nrequests are denied rather than served without validation. Deployments that do not set\n`--enable-workspaces` retain the artifacts-only proxy's current behavior.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Flag composition: allow `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` to be set together;\n  require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` in that combination and fail server startup\n  with a clear error if it is missing.\n- Initialize only the workspace provider in the combined mode; keep tracking and model\n  registry stores uninitialized and non-artifact API endpoints disabled.\n- Per-request workspace validation in the artifact serving path: resolve the artifact path\n  to its parent experiment (S3-scoped `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` layout per the\n  path-resolvability findings), look up the experiment's workspace assignment, and delegate\n  authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure.\n- Deny requests failing workspace validation with HTTP 403 and an error message indicating\n  insufficient workspace permissions.\n- Fail-closed behavior: deny artifact requests with HTTP 503 indicating workspace\n  validation is unavailable when the workspace store is unreachable.\n- Backwards compatibility: omitting either flag preserves current behavior; no existing API\n  contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys change meaning.\n- Measure and document the per-request overhead of workspace resolution (workspace store\n  lookup plus auth plugin validation) to inform the deferred caching decision — performance\n  optimization itself is out of scope (separate RFE).\n- Document limitations for non-standard artifact roots and non-S3 storage backends.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given the artifacts-only instance started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces\n  --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` and a user who is a member of workspace A, when the\n  user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A, then the artifact is\n  served (HTTP 200 with correct artifact content).\n- Given the same deployment and a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B,\n  when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B, then the\n  request is denied (HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient workspace\n  permissions).\n- Given `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, when any authenticated user\n  requests an artifact, then it is served without workspace validation (HTTP 200 regardless\n  of workspace membership — current behavior preserved).\n- Given the workspace-aware artifacts-only instance running and the workspace store\n  (Kubernetes API) unreachable, when a user requests an artifact, then the request is\n  denied (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable — fail-closed).\n- Given `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` set without `--workspace-store-uri`, the\n  server fails to start with a clear error message.\n- Per-request workspace validation overhead is measured and documented.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and\n  `--workspace-store-uri` — direct coverage.\n- HLR-2 (P0): artifact requests validated against workspace membership, 403 on\n  non-membership — direct coverage.\n- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed when the workspace store is unreachable — direct coverage.\n- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant / no-workspace deployments retain current behavior — direct\n  coverage; same flag-gating and initialization code path as HLR-1, so it is implemented in\n  this epic rather than split.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Size: estimated 1-2 sprints; at the top of the range this epic runs above the ~2-week\n  guideline. It is kept as one epic because flag composition, workspace-provider\n  initialization, per-request validation, and fail-closed handling edit the same server\n  initialization sequence and artifact request path. Re-split along\n  initialization-composition vs. request-validation lines if implementation exceeds the\n  2-sprint estimate or the validation logic grows materially beyond the S3-scoped path\n  resolution.\n- The strategy's Architecture Review is pending human review; the fail-closed security\n  design defined here is the primary subject of that review.\n- Workspace validation depends on the plugin resolution working store-free (delivered by\n  the plugin extension work) and on the path-resolvability findings holding.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":81840,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bc9990585c0fc227","agent_id":"a9ee74de494750547","session_id":"40ac0a95-f7b4-4f3c-bf55-b2c4e97b3f62","uuid":"e8a28e1f-90b9-4b