or optional dependencies.\n- Operator validation: reject MLflow CR configurations that enable the split artifacts-only\n  instance without the workspace flags, so misconfiguration cannot result in artifact\n  requests bypassing workspace validation (risk mitigation from the strategy).\n- Verify the operator's Helm chart rendering supports the second Deployment without\n  structural changes to the chart architecture (strategy assumption).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given an MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving enabled, when the operator\n  reconciles the CR, then it creates a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and\n  HTTPRoute separate from the main tracking Deployment, measured by the presence of\n  distinct Deployment resources with the correct flag combinations.\n- The artifacts-only Deployment's container command runs with `--artifacts-only\n  --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth\n  --serve-artifacts`, measured by inspecting the container command in the rendered\n  Deployment spec.\n- The operator rejects (with a clear validation error) configurations that enable the\n  dedicated artifacts-only instance without the workspace flags.\n- The HTTPRoute for the artifacts-only instance is created only when the Gateway API CRD\n  is present at runtime, consistent with the operator's conditional discovery pattern.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-5 (P1): operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment\n  with its own Service and HTTPRoute — direct coverage.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- The HTTPRoute design follows the routing-design investigation's selected approach; if\n  that outcome changes the ingress design, this epic's HTTPRoute scope is rewritten\n  accordingly.\n- The rendered flag combination references the server-side composability capability; this\n  epic's deliverable is the rendered Deployment spec, which is verifiable independently —\n  end-to-end behavior is valid