{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b35f7c7ed92fedf7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tepic_count: 10\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8009 — Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n10\t\n11\tTriage (Step 0): not below-threshold (M-L effort, 4-7 sprints; three affected components;\n12\tmultiple architecturally distinct work streams: MLflow server, MLflow Operator split-server\n13\ttopology, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins extension, plus cross-component integration validation)\n14\tand not documentation-only (all components require code changes). Full decomposition follows.\n15\t\n16\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides (Step 1.5)\n17\t\n18\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. Three overrides\n19\twere applied and are traceable in the epic scope below:\n20\t\n21\t1. **No new flag — combine existing flags.** The AI-generated proposal for a new\n22\t   `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag and `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field\n23\t   is superseded: the decomposition implements composability of the existing\n24\t   `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` flags. No epic introduces a new server flag\n25\t   or a workspace-aware CR field.\n26\t2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle.** The operator epics deploy a\n27\t   dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and\n28\t   HTTPRoute and configure artifact traffic routing — a split-server topology, not a single\n29\t   Helm values propagation.\n30\t3. **SSAR detail is over-specified.** No epic prescribes SelfSubjectAccessReview or other\n31\t   Kubernetes API mechanics for workspace authorization; the plugin epic reuses the existing\n32\t   auth plugin infrastructure, and the kube-rbac-proxy / `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup\n33\t   is explicitly unchanged.\n34\t\n35\t## Epic List\n36\t\n37\t| ID | Title | Type | Component | Team | Priority |\n38\t|----|-------|------|-----------|------|----------|\n39\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Validate plugin workspace-resolution independence from tracking store initialization | Investigation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P0 |\n40\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Validate artifact-path-to-workspace resolvability in the artifacts-only server | Investigation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P0 |\n41\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Extend mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution for the artifacts-only context | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P0 |\n42\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P0 |\n43\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Determine split-server artifact traffic routing design | Investigation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P1 |\n44\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | Operator deployment of the dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P1 |\n45\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E007 | Operator configuration of artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P1 |\n46\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E008 | Determine the audit logging channel for workspace access denials | Investigation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P2 |\n47\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E009 | Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P2 |\n48\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E010 | End-to-end integration validation of workspace-aware split-server artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P1 |\n49\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E011 | Tracking-server artifact delegation configuration (conditional, branch B) | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P1 |\n50\t\n51\t10 main-plan epics plus 1 conditional branch epic. Branch epics are excluded from the\n52\tmain-plan critical path and are not standalone Jira issues; BRANCH-B-E011 is attached to\n53\tthe gating investigation (E005) as a plan document.\n54\t\n55\t## Dependency DAG\n56\t\n57\t```mermaid\n58\tgraph TD\n59\t    E001[\"E001 INV: plugin init independence\"]\n60\t    E002[\"E002 INV: path resolvability\"]\n61\t    E003[\"E003 IMPL: plugin extension\"]\n62\t    E004[\"E004 IMPL: server workspace-aware artifacts-only\"]\n63\t    E005[\"E005 INV: routing design\"]\n64\t    E006[\"E006 IMPL: dedicated instance deployment\"]\n65\t    E007[\"E007 IMPL: artifact traffic routing\"]\n66\t    E008[\"E008 INV: audit channel\"]\n67\t    E009[\"E009 IMPL: audit logging\"]\n68\t    E010[\"E010 IMPL: E2E integration validation\"]\n69\t    E011B[\"BRANCH-B-E011 IMPL: server delegation config (conditional)\"]\n70\t\n71\t    E001 --> E003\n72\t    E002 --> E004\n73\t    E003 --> E004\n74\t    E005 --> E006\n75\t    E005 --> E007\n76\t    E005 --> E011B\n77\t    E004 --> E009\n78\t    E008 --> E009\n79\t    E004 --> E010\n80\t    E006 --> E010\n81\t    E007 --> E010\n82\t```\n83\t\n84\tCritical path (main plan): E001 → E003 → E004 → E010 (4 epics).\n85\t\n86\t## DAG Justification\n87\t\n88\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n89\t|------|------|-----------|\n90\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The plugin-coupling finding rewrites the plugin epic's scope (verify-only vs. refactor of the initialization path). True gate: `gated_by=E001`, `gate_failure_impact=rewrite`. |\n91\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 3 | The path-resolvability finding determines the server's workspace-resolution approach; if paths do not carry resolvable experiment/run identity, the approach is redesigned. True gate: `gated_by=E002`, `gate_failure_impact=rewrite`. |\n92\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 12/13 (framework/library → consumer) | The server's workspace validation builds against the plugin's workspace resolution (loaded via the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point); the consumer serializes after the library it calls. |\n93\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 3 | The routing-design outcome determines the HTTPRoute design for the dedicated instance. True gate: `gated_by=E005`, `gate_failure_impact=rewrite`. |\n94\t| E005 → E007 | Rule 3 | The routing mechanism (gateway-level vs. server-side delegation) determines what the routing epic implements. True gate: `gated_by=E005`, `gate_failure_impact=rewrite`. |\n95\t| E005 → BRANCH-B-E011 | Rule 3 (conditional branch) | The branch epic exists only in outcome B (tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations). `branch=B`, `gated_by=E005`, `gate_failure_impact.action=remove`. |\n96\t| E004 → E009 | Rule 13 | Audit logging extends the workspace-validation denial points the server work creates (same code surface, built after it exists). |\n97\t| E008 → E009 | Rule 3 | The channel decision (server log vs. OTLP vs. Kubernetes audit log) determines the audit epic's scope. True gate: `gated_by=E008`, `gate_failure_impact=rewrite`. |\n98\t| E004 → E010 | Verification ordering | End-to-end validation exercises the workspace-aware server capability; it cannot run before the capability exists. |\n99\t| E006 → E010, E007 → E010 | Verification ordering | End-to-end validation exercises the operator-deployed split-server topology (dedicated instance + routing). |\n100\t| No edge: E004 ∥ E006/E007 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared artifact) | The operator renders flag combinations into a Deployment spec — a configuration reference to the server's capability, not a build-against artifact. The coupling (\"dedicated instance runs the workspace-aware flag combination\") is enforced by the operator epic's own acceptance criterion on the rendered container command, never by a DAG edge. |\n101\t| No edge: E006 ∥ E007 | Rule 15 (same repo, different areas) | New artifacts-only chart templates vs. modification of the main tracking deployment's routing configuration; merge conflicts are coordination risk, not dependency. |\n102\t| No edge: E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E005 ∥ E008 | Independent investigations | Four distinct unknowns resolved by different experiments (plugin source analysis, server artifact-path analysis, routing design analysis, PM/Security channel decision); each produces a different deliverable, so they are not combined. |\n103\t\n104\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n105\t\n106\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n107\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n108\t| HLR-1: `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | |\n109\t| HLR-2: artifact requests validated against workspace membership; 403 on non-membership | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | |\n110\t| HLR-3: fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | |\n111\t| HLR-4: single-tenant / no-workspace deployments retain current behavior | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Same priority as HLR-1..3; same flag-gating and initialization code path — one epic, no split needed. |\n112\t| HLR-5: operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only instance (Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute) | P1 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | |\n113\t| HLR-6: main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | P1 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E007 (+ RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E011 in outcome B) | |\n114\t| HLR-7: auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context without tracking store init | P1 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | E003 is P0 by priority inheritance: it transitively enables the P0 server HLRs 1-3. |\n115\t| HLR-8: audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs | P2 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E009 | Split from the P0 server work rather than bundled: it is a deferrable feature with independent value (audit trail), and its scope is undetermined pending the channel decision — bundling would couple P0 delivery to a PM/Security decision. The split epic extends the denial path after it exists. |\n116\t\n117\tFull coverage: every HLR (P0/P1/P2) maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n118\t\n119\t## Open Question Disposition\n120\t\n121\t| Strategy unknown | Disposition |\n122\t|------------------|-------------|\n123\t| OQ: plugin workspace-store initialization vs. tracking store availability (MLflow Team) | Investigation E001 — answer changes plugin epic scope (verify vs. refactor). |\n124\t| OQ: operator artifact traffic routing design; server-side change needed? (MLflow Team) | Investigation E005 — answer changes HTTPRoute design and conditionally adds the server-side delegation epic (branch B). Bounded outcomes: (A) gateway-level routing only, (B) server delegation change needed. |\n125\t| OQ: audit channel for workspace access denials (PM/Security) | Investigation E008 — answer changes audit epic scope. |\n126\t| OQ: acceptable latency overhead per artifact request (PM/Engineering) | Acceptance criterion on E004 (measure and document per-request overhead), not an investigation: performance optimization of artifact serving is explicitly out of scope (separate RFE), so the answer changes no epic's existence or approach here. See Health Warnings. |\n127\t| Assumption: flags composable without breaking individual-flag initialization | Folded into E004 scope/ACs (implementation detail the team resolves doing the work) with E002 checking the interaction constraints. |\n128\t| Assumption: workspace store provides experiment-to-workspace mapping without tracking stores | Investigation E001 (same experiment as the plugin-coupling question). |\n129\t| Assumption: single-tenant deployments don't set `--enable-workspaces` | Regression scenario in E004 ACs and E010. |\n130\t| Assumption: operator Helm chart supports a second Deployment without structural changes | Folded into E006 scope (chart architecture already renders multiple resource kinds; the epic verifies the second Deployment renders). |\n131\t| Assumption: artifact request paths carry experiment/run identity | Investigation E002 — if false, the server's resolution approach is redesigned. |\n132\t\n133\t## Health Warnings\n134\t\n135\t- **Epic size (E004)**: the strategy estimates the server-side flag composition and workspace\n136\t  validation at 1-2 sprints; at the top of that range the epic runs above the ~2-week\n137\t  guideline. It is kept as one epic because flag composition, workspace-provider\n138\t  initialization, per-request validation, and fail-closed handling all edit the same server\n139\t  initialization sequence and artifact request path — splitting would double-touch the same\n140\t  call sites. **Re-split trigger**: re-split along initialization-composition vs.\n141\t  request-validation lines if implementation exceeds the 2-sprint estimate or the validation\n142\t  logic grows materially beyond the S3-scoped `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` path\n143\t  resolution.\n144\t- **E010 priority**: the end-to-end validation epic is P1, not P0. Its gating deliverable is\n145\t  verification of the P1 operator topology (HLR-5/6); the P0 server behaviors carry their own\n146\t  acceptance scenarios (strategy ACs 1-4) inside E004. Labeling E010 P0 would force the P1\n147\t  operator epics to P0 through priority inheritance, misrepresenting the strategy's priority\n148\t  labels.\n149\t- **Component vocabulary mapping**: `.context/rhai-components.txt` has no entries for\n150\t  \"MLflow Operator\" or \"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\"; every epic uses the canonical name\n151\t  **MLflow** (the closest canonical name covering this component family, owned by the same\n152\t  MLflow Team). `submit.py` would silently strip non-canonical names. Non-obvious mappings:\n153\t  MLflow Operator → MLflow; mlflow-kubernetes-plugins → MLflow.\n154\t- **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins has no architecture-context file** (`.context/architecture-context/`\n155\t  contains mlflow.md, mlflow-operator.md, kube-rbac-proxy.md, PLATFORM.md only). The strategy's\n156\t  dependency table marks it internal and existing, and mlflow.md documents the\n157\t  `kubernetes-auth` plugin (v1.5.0), the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point, and the\n158\t  workspace store — so no onboarding/provisioning chain was generated — but the plugin's\n159\t  current initialization path is unverified by architecture context. E001 exists precisely to\n160\t  validate it; treat plugin-side claims as strategy-asserted until E001 resolves.\n161\t- **Latency/caching tension**: the risk table proposes short-TTL caching of workspace\n162\t  membership decisions, while Out-of-Scope excludes performance optimization of artifact\n163\t  serving (separate RFE). The decomposition follows the out-of-scope boundary: no caching\n164\t  epic; E004 measures and documents per-request validation overhead so the follow-up RFE (or\n165\t  a launch-blocking decision from the open latency question) has data.\n166\t- **Strategy-level prerequisites pending human review** (Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build\n167\t  Onboarding, Licence Validation, Accelerator/Package Support, Documentation Support, UXD\n168\t  Support, Performance Team Support). No onboarding epics were generated: mlflow and\n169\t  mlflow-operator are in the platform with existing Konflux pipelines per architecture\n170\t  context; no new repos or images are introduced. The pending Architecture Review is\n171\t  reflected in E004's human-process-gate signal.\n172\t- **Design doc / ADR \"to be created\"** per the strategy's Supporting Documentation; the\n173\t  routing-design investigation (E005) is the natural input to that design doc.\n174\t\n175\t## Tiered Delivery\n176\t\n177\tNo external dependency has uncertain timing — the strategy's dependency table lists all four\n178\tdependencies as internal and existing (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API,\n179\tkube-rbac-proxy). No Tier 1/Tier 2 split is required. Note, however, a natural phased\n180\tdelivery emerges from the DAG: the server-side capability (E001-E004) delivers independent\n181\tpartial value for manually configured deployments, while the operator to